"{\n  \"judge_type\": \"hybrid_rag\",\n  \"question\": \"Based on Extent of occurence (EOO)/Area of occupancy (AOO) solely, how would you classify the species in the uploaded assessment document (Critically Endangered, Endangered or Vulnerable)?\",\n  \"relevance_score\": 2,\n  \"coverage_score\": 1,\n  \"focus_score\": 2,\n  \"covered_aspects\": [],\n  \"missing_aspects\": [\n    \"EOO value for the species\",\n    \"AOO value for the species\",\n    \"IUCN threshold values for CR, EN, VU based on EOO/AOO\",\n    \"Direct classification decision using those thresholds\"\n  ],\n  \"item_labels\": [\n    {\n      \"rank\": 1,\n      \"block type\": \"text\",\n      \"source\": \"RedListGuidelines.pdf\",\n      \"page\": 75,\n      \"section\": \"8. Guidelines for Applying Criterion D\",\n      \"text\": \". If any significant or plausible threats can be identified, then a full assessment will be necessary to determine the most appropriate classification (e.g., Critically Endangered under criteria B or C, or Vulnerable under criterion D2). If, despite searches, the taxon has not been recorded since the collection of the type specimen, and there are threats in the area, a listing of Critically Endangered (Possibly Extinct) or Extinct may be appropriate (see section 11 for guidance on how to make this determination). 8.2 Example of applying criterion D\",\n      \"reason\": \"Discusses criteria D and threat assessment, but does not provide EOO/AOO values or thresholds relevant to the specific classification request.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rank\": 2,\n      \"block type\": \"text\",\n      \"source\": \"RedListGuidelines.pdf\",\n      \"page\": 11,\n      \"section\": \"Figure 2.1. Structure of the IUCN Red List Categories\",\n      \"text\": \". A taxon is presumed Extinct in the Wild when exhaustive surveys ... CRITICALLY ENDANGERED (CR) A taxon is Critically Endangered when the best available evidence indicates that it meets any of the criteria A to E for Critically Endangered, and it is therefore considered to be facing an extremely high risk of extinction in the wild. ENDANGERED (EN) A taxon is Endangered when the best available evidence indicates that it meets any of the criteria A to E for Endangered, and it is therefore considered to be facing a very high risk of extinction in the wild\",\n      \"reason\": \"Provides generic definitions of categories but contains no EOO/AOO data or specific thresholds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rank\": 3,\n      \"block type\": \"text\",\n      \"source\": \"RedListGuidelines.pdf\",\n      \"page\": 15,\n      \"section\": \"2.3 Nature of the criteria\",\n      \"text\": \". Only the criteria for the highest category of threat that the taxon qualifies for should be listed... Although the criteria for each of the categories of threat are based on quantitative thresholds, the system remains relatively flexible to ensure that taxa for which there is very little information can also be assessed\",\n      \"reason\": \"Mentions quantitative thresholds in abstract, but does not supply the EOO/AOO thresholds needed for the question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rank\": 4,\n      \"block type\": \"text\",\n      \"source\": \"RL_categories_and_criteria.pdf\",\n      \"page\": 37,\n      \"section\": \"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species™\",\n      \"text\": \"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species ™ ... Species are assigned to one of eight categories of threat based on whether they meet criteria linked to population trend, population size and structure and geographic range. Species listed as Critically Endangered, Endangered or Vulnerable are collectively described as ‘Threatened’.\",\n      \"reason\": \"General overview of the Red List; no specific EOO/AOO figures or classification guidance for the target species.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The retrieved set consists of general IUCN guideline excerpts that describe categories and criteria but provide no EOO/AOO values or the thresholds needed to assign a specific threat category to the species. Consequently, relevance and coverage are low, and the set contains considerable irrelevant material for the asked classification.\"\n}"